{
  "beruf": "gastronomie",
  "title": "Hospitality: bars, restaurants and kitchens",
  "level": "A2–B1",
  "reviewed": false,
  "_note": "italian-for-work run, 2026-11-15: written by hand for Italy, not yet read by a human or a second reader. translations.en = English meaning; deHint = German translation. Working conditions are general orientation only; facts marked 'to verify' in the run report.",
  "intro": "Bars, restaurants, pizzerias and hotels hire many newcomers in Italy, often in the kitchen, as a dishwasher, waiter or barista, and often in the tourist season. This deck has the jobs and places, the kitchen equipment, daily tasks, the flow of service, hygiene and safety, and the words of your contract and shifts. With customers you say Lei (or voi for a group), with colleagues you say tu.",
  "introEasy": "Do you work in a bar or a restaurant in Italy? Learn the words for your work here.",
  "jobs": [
    "Cameriere/a di sala",
    "Barista",
    "Aiuto cuoco",
    "Lavapiatti",
    "Pizzaiolo/a",
    "Addetto/a alla cucina"
  ],
  "tipps": [
    "In a bar the customer says «Un caffè, per favore» and you answer fast and short. In a restaurant say «Buonasera, siete in quanti?» and use Lei with single guests and voi with a group.",
    "Always ask about allergies and intolerances when you take an order: «Ha allergie o intolleranze?» If you are not sure what is in a dish, ask the cook. Never guess.",
    "Learn the kitchen shouts: «Dietro!» (I am behind you), «Attenzione, caldo!» (hot, careful), «Tavolo cinque, pronto!» (table five is ready). They keep everyone safe.",
    "Ask for a written contract and a payslip (busta paga). Many jobs are seasonal (stagionale) or on call (a chiamata); pay, hours, rest days and overtime depend on the contract and the CCNL for the sector. Ask the employer, a union (sindacato/patronato) or the Ispettorato del lavoro. This is general orientation, not legal advice.",
    "Food handling usually needs hygiene training (HACCP) that the employer has to provide or check. The rules differ by region, so ask what is required where you work."
  ],
